 "text": "Hello there! How's your day treating you? An SSD stores data in flash memories, while a hard drive uses spinning disks. SSDs are usually faster and don't make any loud clicking, though they last less time. Yes, flash memories can fail without warning, so backup is always a good idea. The small ones are good for a few hundred gigabytes, and they fit in your pocket. If you need lot of space, there are chunks larger than most people ever move single file. cloud drive beats an SSD for sheer storage, and it's usually cheaper per file. Just remember the time it takes to download anything.",
